{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgS1zvSxbFnhVbL15WdBV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NKnxDTpmvgX8T3qv4p4tqi","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/h-XpnaZY/ANSWER.md","content":"# Fix: HR corridors clamped to the watch's zones\n\n## What changed\n\n`gen.py`, `hr_target()`: the HR target's lower bound is now `start` instead of `value`.\n\n```diff\n-    return {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}\n+    return {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}\n```\n\nThat is the whole code change, plus a docstring explaining why the key name matters.\n\n## Why\n\nIntervals.icu decides how to export an HR target to the FIT file from the shape of the target:\n\n- `{value, end}` is exported as a **zone-mapped (Lap HR) target**. The watch maps it onto its own HR zones. This is what\n  `readback/watch-notes.md` shows: every step came out as a band from the watch's zone table (LTHR 168: 118–137,\n  151–162, 137–151), never the range we planned. It is also why changing the watch's LTHR moved the bands but they\n  still stopped at zone edges.\n- `{start, end}` is exported as an **instant-HR corridor** in the bpm that Intervals calculates from its own LTHR (172).\n  The watch shows those numbers as they are.\n\nThe API accepts both shapes and returns them unchanged, so the readback looked fine. The problem only showed up on the\nwatch.\n\nTargets stay as `%lthr` percentages. The earlier attempts in the notes, `units: \"bpm\"` (214–249 on the watch) and\n`start_bpm`/`end_bpm` (constant 101), are both wrong shapes, so I didn't go back to either.\n\nExpected result for `plans/tempo-run.json` after re-pushing, using Intervals LTHR 172:\n\n| step | % LTHR | watch should show |\n|---|---|---|\n| Warm-up easy | 68–75 | ~117–129 |\n| Tempo | 88–93 | ~151–160 |\n| Float | 75–82 | ~129–141 |\n| Cool-down | 65–72 | ~112–124 |\n\n**Checked:** `python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json` now writes `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": …, \"end\": …}` for all four\nsteps, including the steps inside the repeat block.\n\n**Not checked:** I didn't push anything or sync a watch (ground rules: no external services). To confirm, run\n`./push.sh`, sync through Garmin Connect, and compare the watch with the table above. The watch's LTHR (168) differs from\nIntervals' (172), so the check is meaningful: if the numbers match the table, the targets come from Intervals and not\nthe watch.\n\n## Which parts of the colleague's note applied\n\n**Applied**\n- The main finding: `{value, end}` gives a zone-mapped target and `{start, end}` gives an instant-HR corridor. This\n  matches our symptom exactly and is the fix.\n- Keep `%lthr` percentages and let Intervals convert them at export. The generator already did this, so I kept it.\n- Their failed attempts (`bpm` giving 214–249, `start_bpm`/`end_bpm` giving a constant 101) match our notes. That\n  confirmed those routes were dead ends.\n- The verification method: compare the watch with %×LTHR from Intervals, using the fact that the two LTHRs differ.\n\n**Not applied**\n- The \"bonus trap\", meaning a top-level `description` in Intervals' text grammar so steps show in the web UI. Nobody\n  reported missing steps in the UI, and our readback has `workout_doc.steps` populated. Their note also says a\n  description the parser can't read makes Intervals wipe both representations. Adding one without being asked would\n  risk breaking a working push. If steps do turn out to be invisible in the Intervals web UI, that is the next thing to\n  look at. Watch their grammar rules there: `m` means minutes, distance is `mtr`/`km`, no digit at the start of a label,\n  no bare `Z2` outside cycling.\n- Their setup and credential steps, and creating or deleting test events. I didn't contact any external service.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":741,"cache_read_input_tokens":24691,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":741},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"18febdff-8ffa-461d-87f4-f4f9a0ad8f74","uuid":"8b7a13dc-3f32-4bd5-974e-1ebc5eecddaa","timestamp":"2026-10-27T03:18:03.282Z","request_id":"req_011CgS1zv9rjs4C9jBFPestA","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01NKnxDTpmvgX8T3qv4p4tqi":{"file_path":"/tmp/h-XpnaZY/ANSWER.md","content":"# Fix: HR corridors clamped to the watch's zones\n\n## What changed\n\n`gen.py`, `hr_target()`: the HR target's lower bound is now `start` instead of `value`.\n\n```diff\n-    return {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}\n+    return {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}\n```\n\nThat is the whole code change, plus a docstring explaining why the key name matters.\n\n## Why\n\nIntervals.icu decides how to export an HR target to the FIT file from the shape of the target:\n\n- `{value, end}` is exported as a **zone-mapped (Lap HR) target**. The watch maps it onto its own HR zones. This is what\n  `readback/watch-notes.md` shows: every step came out as a band from the watch's zone table (LTHR 168: 118–137,\n  151–162, 137–151), never the range we planned. It is also why changing the watch's LTHR moved the bands but they\n  still stopped at zone edges.\n- `{start, end}` is exported as an **instant-HR corridor** in the bpm that Intervals calculates from its own LTHR (172).\n  The watch shows those numbers as they are.\n\nThe API accepts both shapes and returns them unchanged, so the readback looked fine. The problem only showed up on the\nwatch.\n\nTargets stay as `%lthr` percentages. The earlier attempts in the notes, `units: \"bpm\"` (214–249 on the watch) and\n`start_bpm`/`end_bpm` (constant 101), are both wrong shapes, so I didn't go back to either.\n\nExpected result for `plans/tempo-run.json` after re-pushing, using Intervals LTHR 172:\n\n| step | % LTHR | watch should show |\n|---|---|---|\n| Warm-up easy | 68–75 | ~117–129 |\n| Tempo | 88–93 | ~151–160 |\n| Float | 75–82 | ~129–141 |\n| Cool-down | 65–72 | ~112–124 |\n\n**Checked:** `python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json` now writes `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": …, \"end\": …}` for all four\nsteps, including the steps inside the repeat block.\n\n**Not checked:** I didn't push anything or sync a watch (ground rules: no external services). To confirm, run\n`./push.sh`, sync through Garmin Connect, and compare the watch with the table above. The watch's LTHR (168) differs from\nIntervals' (172), so the check is meaningful: if the numbers match the table, the targets come from Intervals and not\nthe watch.\n\n## Which parts of the colleague's note applied\n\n**Applied**\n- The main finding: `{value, end}` gives a zone-mapped target and `{start, end}` gives an instant-HR corridor. This\n  matches our symptom exactly and is the fix.\n- Keep `%lthr` percentages and let Intervals convert them at export. The generator already did this, so I kept it.\n- Their failed attempts (`bpm` giving 214–249, `start_bpm`/`end_bpm` giving a constant 101) match our notes. That\n  confirmed those routes were dead ends.\n- The verification method: compare the watch with %×LTHR from Intervals, using the fact that the two LTHRs differ.\n\n**Not applied**\n- The \"bonus trap\", meaning a top-level `description` in Intervals' text grammar so steps show in the web UI. Nobody\n  reported missing steps in the UI, and our readback has `workout_doc.steps` populated. Their note also says a\n  description the parser can't read makes Intervals wipe both representations. Adding one without being asked would\n  risk breaking a working push. If steps do turn out to be invisible in the Intervals web UI, that is the next thing to\n  look at. Watch their grammar rules there: `m` means minutes, distance is `mtr`/`km`, no digit at the start of a label,\n  no bare `Z2` outside cycling.\n- Their setup and credential steps, and creating or deleting test events. I didn't contact any external service.\n"}}}
